efix\n            prefix *= nums[i]\n        suffix = 1\n        for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n            answer[i] *= suffix\n            suffix *= nums[i]\n        return answer\n","approach":"**Prefix products times suffix products.** `answer[i] = (nums[0] * ... * nums[i-1]) * (nums[i+1] * ... * nums[n-1])`. A left-to-right pass stores the left product in `answer[i]`; a right-to-left pass multiplies in the right product using a single running variable. The output array is the only storage, so extra space is O(1).\n\n**Why it works:** the two factors together cover every index except `i` exactly once, and no division is involved so zeros are handled naturally (a zero on either side makes that side's product zero).\n\n**Common mistakes:** dividing the total product by `nums[i]` (breaks on zero and is forbidden); allocating separate prefix and suffix arrays when asked for constant extra space; off-by-one when the running product should be updated after writing, not before.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** How would you handle it if division were allowed? (count zeros: 0 zeros divide, 1 zero only that slot is non-zero, 2+ zeros all zero). What if the products can overflow? (modular arithmetic or big integers, and discuss what the caller wants). Can you do the same for sums? (trivial: total minus element, which is why the product version forbids division).","complexity":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(1) beyond the output"},"similar":["subarray-sum-equals-k","trapping-rain-water"],"number":8,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def productExceptSelf(self, nums: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n        pass\n"}